  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 311 PM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nDepending on when the front departs to the southeast, we'll have to \nkeep an eye on the potential for redevelopment along it Thursday \nafternoon when the National Blend of Models (NBM) gives a 20-35% \nchance for more than 2000 J/kg SBCAPE across our far south. In fact, \nsome guidance suggests the front could lift back north and allow a \nfew showers and storms along/east of roughly I-75 Friday afternoon \nand evening as well. However, for most of our area, we should have a \nbreak Thursday into Friday, with the next system bringing a stronger \nfront and perhaps some shower and storm chances Saturday into Sunday.\n\nPrinciple Components (PCs) for Empirical orthogonal functions (EOFs) \napplied to the Low Resolution Ensemble Forecast (LREF) system's \n500mb heights suggest the primary source of model spread - and \nhence, forecast uncertainty - over the weekend is in the depth of \ntroughing over the Great Lakes and Northeast. Deeper troughing, \nsupported by about 40% of LREF membership, suggests a stronger cold \nfront which would bring the area scattered showers and storms \nfollowed by below normal temperatures; in addition, stronger \nkinematics suggest our area could see at least a low (5+%) risk for \nsevere weather with any storms that develop. A weaker trough to our \neast (the remaining 60% of LREF members favor this solution), by \ncontrast, would favor seasonably warm temperatures through the \nweekend and low chances for precipitation. In either case, at least \na couple days of dry weather can be expected to close the weekend \nand kick off next work week as northwest flow overspreads the local \narea in the wake of that trough.",
